---
source: crates/mrq-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: "stdout_of(&[name, \"--help\"])"
---
Combine several files into one document

Usage: mrq merge [OPTIONS] [PATH]...

Arguments:
  [PATH]...
          Files, directories, globs, or `-` for stdin

Options:
      --shift <N>
          Push each file's headings down by N levels (default: one per heading level above them, the section headings and the title)

      --title <TITLE>
          Title of the merged document

      --no-wrap
          Concatenate without wrapping each file in a section heading

      --frontmatter <MODE>
          How to combine frontmatter

          Possible values:
          - keep-first: Keep the first file's frontmatter only
          - merge:      Merge all frontmatter; the first file wins on conflicts
          - strip:      Drop all frontmatter
          
          [default: merge]

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write to this file instead of stdout

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --config <PATH>
          Use this config file instead of discovering `.mrq.yaml`

      --flavor <FLAVOR>
          Markdown flavor [default: frontmatter `mrq.flavor`, then config, then gfm]

          Possible values:
          - commonmark: Strict CommonMark
          - gfm:        GitHub Flavored Markdown
          - obsidian:   Obsidian: gfm + wikilinks, embeds, callouts, tags, math, comments
          - mkdocs:     MkDocs: gfm + admonitions, heading attributes, definition lists, math

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colors
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Diagnostics output format [default: human, or github when GITHUB_ACTIONS=true]

          Possible values:
          - human:  Human-readable, with source snippets
          - json
          - ndjson: One JSON object per line
          - sarif:  SARIF 2.1.0
          - github: GitHub Actions workflow annotations

  -q, --quiet...
          Print less output; repeat for even less

  -v, --verbose...
          Print more output; repeat for even more
